{#- Pronunciation: heteronyms read by meaning, and Sounds Like rules with their syntax. Claims 21 to 23, plan section 5;
    the 96 to 98 in 100 figure is in the fact sheet. The Sound Key table comes from the app's own key
    (SoundsLikeRespelling.key in the Listen2 repo), copied into src/_data/listen2Voices.json as soundKey: keep the two
    in step. No accuracy figure for words by meaning: none is in the fact sheet. -#}
    <!-- Pronunciation -->
    <section id="pronunciation" style="max-width: 1200px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 88px 24px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 40px" aria-labelledby="l2-pron">
      <div style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 56px; align-items: flex-start">
        <div style="flex: 1 1 460px; min-width: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px">
          <div style="font-family: 'Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono', monospace; font-size: 13px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #38B6FF">Pronunciation</div>
          <h2 id="l2-pron" style="margin: 0; font-size: clamp(32px, 4vw, 48px); line-height: 1.1; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.02em">Same spelling, different word</h2>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 19px; line-height: 1.6; color: #D4D4D0">English is full of heteronyms: words spelled the same but said two ways, depending on what they mean. You read a book yesterday and will read another tomorrow. A lead pipe has nothing to do with the lead in a play, and a tear in your eye is not a tear in your sleeve. Listen2 Voices read each heteronym by its meaning in the sentence, and an accented spelling such as résumé is read as the noun, not the verb.</p>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.6; color: #A3A3A0">The setting is called Read Words by Their Meaning, under Pronunciation.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="flex: 1 1 420px; min-width: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px">
          <h3 style="margin: 0; font-size: 26px; line-height: 1.25; font-weight: 700">Tell the voice exactly how to say it</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.6; color: #A3A3A0">Names, place names and the words of your own field are the usual trouble spots. Most voices can only be given a different spelling and left to guess again. The Listen2 Voices take pronunciation instructions instead. A Sounds Like rule spells the word by its sounds, the way a dictionary or a newspaper spells out a name, and a Listen2 voice says exactly those sounds. Every document follows the rule from then on.</p>
          <div style="border: 1px solid #2A2A2D; border-radius: 20px; background: #131315; padding: 22px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px">
            <div style="font-family: 'Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono', monospace; font-size: 13px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #A3A3A0">Sounds Like rules</div>
            <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 21px; line-height: 1.5">Saoirse <span style="color: #A3A3A0">sounds like</span> <code style="font-family: 'Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono', monospace; color: #FF66C4">SEER-shuh</code></p>
            <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 21px; line-height: 1.5">Nguyen <span style="color: #A3A3A0">sounds like</span> <code style="font-family: 'Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono', monospace; color: #FF66C4">nuh-WIN</code></p>
            <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 21px; line-height: 1.5">Nike <span style="color: #A3A3A0">sounds like</span> <code style="font-family: 'Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono', monospace; color: #FF66C4">NY-kee</code></p>
          </div>
          <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.55; color: #A3A3A0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px">
            <li>Put a hyphen between the parts of a word.</li>
            <li>Write the stressed part in capitals. A word with one part needs none: red, win.</li>
            <li>Put spaces between words: <code style="font-family: 'Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono', monospace; color: #F5F5F2">MAR-ee AN</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.55; color: #A3A3A0">Each spelling stands for one phoneme, the single sound it names in the key below, so SEER-shuh is the four sounds /s ɪɹ ʃ ə/ with the stress on the first part. Given the sounds of a word, the voices said those sounds in 96 to 98 of 100 cases in our tests. Other voices follow the rule too, reading a plain-word version of your spelling.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <details class="l2-measured" style="border-top: 1px solid #2A2A2D; border-bottom: 1px solid #2A2A2D; padding: 8px 0">
        <summary>The Sound Key: what to type for each sound</summary>
        <p style="margin: 8px 0 0">The same key is in the app, behind Sound Key in the rule form. The middle column is the phoneme in the International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA).</p>
        <div class="l2-keys">
          {%- for group in [["Vowels", listen2Voices.soundKey.vowels], ["Consonants", listen2Voices.soundKey.consonants]] %}
          <table class="l2-key">
            <caption style="text-align: left; font-size: 17px; font-weight: 700; color: #F5F5F2; padding-bottom: 8px">{{ group[0] }}</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">IPA</th><th scope="col">As in</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {%- for k in group[1] %}
              <tr><td>{{ k.type }}</td><td>{{ k.ipa }}</td><td>{{ k.asIn }}</td></tr>
              {%- endfor %}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          {%- endfor %}
        </div>
        <p style="margin: 16px 0 0">"uh" and "er" sound full in the stressed part (cup, bird) and light everywhere else (about, butter). "y" before a vowel is the y in yes; anywhere else it is the vowel in my. Always write g for the g in go and j for the j in jam. A doubled letter is one sound, and x is "ks".</p>
      </details>
    </section>
